WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.760
You know, when you sit down at your computer in the morning, coffee in hand, completely ready to tackle a big project.

00:07.240 --> 00:07.360
Right.

00:07.580 --> 00:15.660
And instead of actually doing your work, you feel like you are just constantly, I don't know, micromanaging this glowing rectangle.

00:15.760 --> 00:16.560
Oh, absolutely.

00:16.920 --> 00:20.980
I mean, I'm talking about the sheer physical labor of just using a modern computer.

00:21.320 --> 00:29.940
You open an app, you drag a window to the side of the screen, you click export, you hunt for the file in your download folder.

00:30.100 --> 00:31.240
And it's never where you think it is.

00:31.380 --> 00:31.760
Exactly.

00:32.020 --> 00:38.940
You drag it to the desktop, you open a browser tab, you attach it to an email, you really enter files too big, so you, you know, open another app to compress it.

00:39.000 --> 00:39.220
Yeah.

00:39.960 --> 00:42.840
You aren't really creating anything in those moments.

00:43.420 --> 00:48.180
You are acting as this, like, exhausted middle manager of your own digital life.

00:48.180 --> 00:53.340
It is a very specific, pervasive kind of cognitive fatigue.

00:53.560 --> 00:57.920
And I think most of us just accept it as the cost of doing business in the 21st century.

00:58.020 --> 00:58.100
Right.

00:58.180 --> 00:59.500
We just think that's how computers work.

00:59.500 --> 00:59.940
Yeah.

01:00.200 --> 01:04.860
But you are essentially providing the connective tissue for the work rather than doing the work itself.

01:05.100 --> 01:07.100
Which is wild when you think about it.

01:07.220 --> 01:07.700
It is.

01:07.820 --> 01:18.460
The machine is sitting there infinitely powerful, but it's completely passive until you, the human, you know, manually coordinate every single one of its moving parts.

01:18.460 --> 01:18.860
I feel that.

01:18.860 --> 01:19.980
I feel that in my bones.

01:19.980 --> 01:26.400
It's like being a babysitter for a dozen incredibly smart but completely helpless toddlers.

01:26.400 --> 01:28.180
That is a perfect way to put it.

01:28.320 --> 01:32.440
Like, if I don't hold their hands and walk them from one room to the next, literally nothing happens.

01:32.600 --> 01:33.420
Nothing happens.

01:33.560 --> 01:37.840
And today, our mission is to completely dismantle that expectation.

01:38.400 --> 01:41.880
We are going to explore a way out of this trap for you.

01:41.880 --> 01:42.200
Yeah.

01:42.320 --> 01:44.200
We have some incredible source material today.

01:44.320 --> 01:44.840
We really do.

01:45.180 --> 01:49.280
We're doing a deep dive into a June 2026 paper by Flyxion.

01:49.860 --> 01:54.940
It's titled, um, computing underscore as underscore flow dot PDF.

01:55.380 --> 01:56.420
Computing as flow.

01:56.420 --> 01:56.860
Right.

01:57.120 --> 02:01.300
And I am telling you, this material is going to permanently change how you look at the screen in front of you.

02:01.420 --> 02:02.260
I really think it will.

02:02.540 --> 02:05.220
We are exploring a massive paradigm shift.

02:05.440 --> 02:06.560
It's called flow computing.

02:07.200 --> 02:17.780
And it's about moving away from being your computer's frantic micromanager and instead becoming, well, an architect of systems that just inherently run themselves.

02:17.980 --> 02:19.120
Which sounds like magic.

02:19.120 --> 02:28.340
But to understand how to fix our modern, everyday digital workflows, this paper argues we actually have to go all the way down to the fundamental hardware level.

02:28.420 --> 02:28.900
Wait, really?

02:29.120 --> 02:29.580
The hardware?

02:29.720 --> 02:30.000
Yeah.

02:30.200 --> 02:34.540
We have to look at how computers process logic at the most microscopic scale.

02:34.680 --> 02:40.420
Because that fatigue you're describing, that middle management exhaustion, it isn't a software bug.

02:40.500 --> 02:42.040
It's an architectural legacy.

02:42.280 --> 02:42.560
Wow.

02:42.560 --> 02:49.580
Specifically, the paper builds on this foundational concept introduced in a 2005 book by Karl Fant.

02:49.740 --> 02:50.460
Okay, Karl Fant.

02:50.560 --> 02:50.700
Right.

02:50.800 --> 02:52.940
The book is called Logically Determined Design.

02:53.080 --> 02:58.660
And he identified this, like, massive foundational flaw in how we think about computing, right?

02:58.880 --> 03:00.820
Starting with just basic Boolean logic.

03:00.940 --> 03:01.360
Exactly.

03:01.500 --> 03:03.860
He calls it the problem of the missing mathematician.

03:04.160 --> 03:05.120
The missing mathematician.

03:05.240 --> 03:05.620
Oh, no, quick.

03:05.640 --> 03:08.900
Okay, you're going to have to explain how a mathematician is missing inside my laptop.

03:09.240 --> 03:11.000
Well, we have to picture a chalkboard.

03:11.320 --> 03:11.640
Okay.

03:11.640 --> 03:13.860
Okay, so, Boolean logic.

03:14.100 --> 03:17.120
That's the fundamental A and D, or R, not T gates, right?

03:17.140 --> 03:17.380
Right.

03:17.580 --> 03:22.680
The stuff that makes up all computing, it is fundamentally just a mathematical symbol system.

03:22.760 --> 03:23.020
Okay.

03:23.240 --> 03:26.900
You have functions, you have symbols, you have rules for how those symbols interact.

03:27.240 --> 03:33.660
But Fant points out this glaring omission in how we built computers based on this math.

03:33.960 --> 03:34.600
Which is what?

03:34.600 --> 03:40.080
On its own, a Boolean logic expression drawn on a chalkboard is completely passive.

03:40.080 --> 03:43.320
It is, in his words, expressionally incomplete.

03:43.680 --> 03:45.680
Because the chalk can't do the math itself.

03:45.820 --> 03:46.240
Exactly.

03:46.640 --> 03:48.040
It requires an active agent.

03:48.380 --> 03:48.960
A mathematician.

03:49.420 --> 03:49.700
Right.

03:50.220 --> 03:52.240
Traditionally, that was a human holding a pencil.

03:52.940 --> 04:00.760
The mathematician is one who looks at the expression, sees that the inputs are ready, applies the rules, erases the old symbols, writes the new symbols.

04:00.760 --> 04:03.720
And moves the data from one function to the next in the right order.

04:03.920 --> 04:04.260
Yes.

04:04.980 --> 04:06.540
The math doesn't coordinate itself.

04:06.680 --> 04:11.860
So the coordination behavior, like the actual when and how things move, that isn't in the math at all.

04:11.940 --> 04:13.760
It is entirely in the mathematician's head.

04:13.900 --> 04:14.140
Right.

04:14.500 --> 04:20.640
Now, when engineers started building physical computers out of silicon, they didn't actually solve this problem.

04:20.760 --> 04:21.300
They didn't.

04:21.300 --> 04:21.700
No.

04:21.960 --> 04:25.640
They just replaced the human mathematician with a mechanical substitute.

04:25.920 --> 04:26.280
Oh, wait.

04:27.260 --> 04:28.100
The clock.

04:28.260 --> 04:28.600
Yes.

04:29.260 --> 04:29.440
Yeah.

04:29.440 --> 04:30.360
The global clock.

04:30.440 --> 04:30.580
Okay.

04:30.680 --> 04:32.420
This is the state transition metaphor.

04:33.080 --> 04:33.560
Exactly.

04:33.740 --> 04:40.400
Because this is the dominant way modern computers work, right down to the CPU you were using to listen to this deep dive right now.

04:40.480 --> 04:40.680
Yeah.

04:40.720 --> 04:41.560
Almost every computer.

04:42.140 --> 04:45.740
You have this central clock ticking away billions of times a second.

04:45.900 --> 04:47.560
I mean, that's what gigahertz actually means, right?

04:47.580 --> 04:47.600
Yeah.

04:47.600 --> 04:48.600
That's exactly what it means.

04:49.020 --> 04:50.480
Billions of ticks per second.

04:50.600 --> 04:54.140
And at every single tick, the computer looks at everything and says, okay, what state are we in now?

04:54.240 --> 04:55.020
Move to the next state.

04:55.620 --> 05:00.500
And the problem is, it's a system forced into arbitrary sequentiality.

05:01.000 --> 05:08.360
The clock dictates the pace of the entire machine, regardless of whether a specific internal process is actually ready to advance or not.

05:08.420 --> 05:09.300
It just ticks anyway.

05:09.420 --> 05:10.120
It just ticks.

05:10.480 --> 05:13.580
And the system samples the inputs, ready or not.

05:13.580 --> 05:18.020
It assumes the, quote unquote, mathematician did the work in time for the bell.

05:18.500 --> 05:19.040
That's wild.

05:19.600 --> 05:22.840
It's like an orchestra, but nobody has any sheet music.

05:23.060 --> 05:24.020
Oh, I like that.

05:24.140 --> 05:26.300
Like the violins don't know when the cellos are playing.

05:26.380 --> 05:28.200
The trumpets don't know what the drums are doing.

05:28.380 --> 05:35.160
And the only way they know when to play their single note is because there is this frantic hyperactive conductor standing at the front.

05:35.220 --> 05:36.280
Just pointing at everyone.

05:36.280 --> 05:43.380
Pointing at every single musician, one by one, screaming, play, play, play on this rigid, unyielding beat.

05:43.620 --> 05:47.420
And if you aren't ready to play when he points at you, the whole symphony crashes.

05:47.620 --> 05:48.700
That is exactly it.

05:48.720 --> 05:55.280
And the cost of maintaining that frantic conductor is what Fant calls the correspondence problem.

05:55.300 --> 05:56.440
The correspondence problem.

05:56.680 --> 05:57.540
Unpack that for me.

05:57.540 --> 06:05.980
Think about a natural computational process, like rendering a 3D video, calculating a massive spreadsheet, or even just opening a web browser.

06:06.240 --> 06:06.500
Okay.

06:06.680 --> 06:10.980
That process has its own natural, organic, concurrent structure.

06:11.560 --> 06:13.440
Some things rely on other things.

06:13.580 --> 06:15.700
Like step B has to wait for step A.

06:16.020 --> 06:16.360
Right.

06:16.360 --> 06:20.100
The lighting calculation in a game has to wait for the geometry calculation.

06:20.480 --> 06:24.300
But other things, like rendering the audio, well, that can happen at the exact same time.

06:24.460 --> 06:25.240
It's organic.

06:25.240 --> 06:27.820
But the global clock doesn't care about organic.

06:27.900 --> 06:29.080
No, it just ticks.

06:29.360 --> 06:29.500
Yeah.

06:29.520 --> 06:37.920
Which means the programmer has to manually align that natural, organic structure with the artificial, rigid, sequential schedule of the global clock.

06:38.180 --> 06:39.200
That sounds like a nightmare.

06:39.480 --> 06:39.900
It is.

06:40.020 --> 06:50.480
They have to do incredibly complex mental gymnastics to make sure every single input for every single microoperation is perfectly ready before that clock ticks.

06:50.620 --> 06:50.920
Wow.

06:50.920 --> 06:56.280
They have to map a multidimensional concurrent reality onto a one-dimensional timeline.

06:56.480 --> 07:00.160
That is just, I mean, it sounds incredibly inefficient and error-prone.

07:00.220 --> 07:03.200
It's the source of so many bugs in software today.

07:03.320 --> 07:04.740
But hold on, let me push back a bit here.

07:04.840 --> 07:05.020
Yeah.

07:05.120 --> 07:09.720
If we get rid of the central clock, aren't you just guaranteeing total chaos?

07:09.960 --> 07:10.560
How so?

07:10.560 --> 07:13.000
Well, how does an orchestra play without a conductor?

07:13.580 --> 07:16.540
If everyone just plays whenever they want, you don't get a symphony, you get noise.

07:17.080 --> 07:21.460
Isn't the global clock, for all its flaws, just simpler to manage?

07:21.780 --> 07:28.040
It seems that way, but Fant's brilliant alternative proves that we actually don't need the conductor to prevent chaos.

07:28.680 --> 07:28.980
Okay.

07:29.460 --> 07:30.340
What's the alternative?

07:30.340 --> 07:35.540
The solution is called null convention logic, or NCL.

07:35.660 --> 07:36.720
Null convention logic.

07:36.780 --> 07:37.020
Yeah.

07:37.780 --> 07:44.440
Fant realized that the way to get rid of the central clock is to encode the coordination directly into the logic itself.

07:44.520 --> 07:47.780
Okay, so to use my analogy, you give the musicians sheet music.

07:47.920 --> 07:48.380
Exactly.

07:48.860 --> 07:51.720
You give them sheet music that tells them exactly who to listen for.

07:52.140 --> 07:57.460
The violinist's sheet music says, do not play until you hear the cello play a C sharp.

07:57.460 --> 08:00.860
Oh, I see. So they don't look at the conductor, they just listen to the environment.

08:01.160 --> 08:10.020
Right. Null convention logic introduces a way for the physical logic gates to distinguish between actual valid data and the absence of data.

08:10.180 --> 08:10.660
The absence.

08:10.880 --> 08:13.480
And that absence is called the NUL state.

08:13.640 --> 08:15.560
Now, traditional computers can't do this.

08:15.620 --> 08:15.920
Why not?

08:16.160 --> 08:23.320
In a normal CPU, a wire either has high voltage or low voltage, a 1 or a 0. Both are considered data.

08:23.320 --> 08:27.900
The CPU doesn't know if the 0 is a real 0 or if the data just hasn't arrived yet.

08:28.380 --> 08:28.760
Oh, wow.

08:29.020 --> 08:29.180
Yeah.

08:29.260 --> 08:32.040
So it just has to trust the clock to tell it when the data is real.

08:32.120 --> 08:32.440
Exactly.

08:32.940 --> 08:34.560
But NCL can tell the difference.

08:35.000 --> 08:42.200
Because it can differentiate between NLL and data, the logic gate itself knows when all its prerequisites have arrived.

08:42.300 --> 08:43.540
Because it's not NLL anymore.

08:43.740 --> 08:43.940
Right.

08:44.220 --> 08:47.580
It literally sits there in an NLL state just waiting.

08:47.580 --> 08:55.380
When valid data arrives on all its input wires, it activates, does its calculation, and passes the result forward.

08:55.540 --> 08:56.760
So it coordinates itself.

08:56.920 --> 08:57.320
Completely.

08:57.560 --> 09:03.060
It flows spontaneously without an external interpreter, without a clock, without a missing mathematician.

09:03.380 --> 09:04.420
That is mind-blowing.

09:04.920 --> 09:10.400
But, okay, if we aren't transitioning from state to state on a clock's beat, what are we actually doing?

09:10.500 --> 09:15.120
Well, the paper introduces a concept that replaces the idea of a computational state entirely.

09:15.360 --> 09:15.600
Okay.

09:15.600 --> 09:17.600
We move from states to wavefronts.

09:18.280 --> 09:18.880
Wavefronts.

09:19.280 --> 09:23.100
Okay, a wavefront is a profoundly different way of visualizing computation.

09:23.360 --> 09:24.020
It really is.

09:24.320 --> 09:29.140
A state is a frozen snapshot of the entire machine at one discrete tick of the clock.

09:29.740 --> 09:32.420
A wavefront, however, is a monotonic transition.

09:32.760 --> 09:34.980
Monotonic meaning it only goes in one direction.

09:35.180 --> 09:35.500
Correct.

09:35.880 --> 09:40.860
It moves from a completely NLL state, meaning no data is present in the circuit,

09:40.860 --> 09:46.420
to a completely data state as the information flows through, and then it resets back to NLL right behind it.

09:46.540 --> 09:51.300
Okay, I'm picturing a wave of water literally washing through a dry riverbed.

09:51.360 --> 09:51.700
Yes.

09:51.920 --> 09:54.100
So the dry riverbed is the NLL state.

09:54.320 --> 09:56.400
The crest of the wave is the data.

09:57.080 --> 10:02.140
And as the wave passes, the riverbed goes dry again, just waiting for the next wave.

10:02.140 --> 10:08.520
That physical visualization is entirely accurate for how the electrons actually move in an NCL circuit.

10:08.740 --> 10:09.380
That's so cool.

10:09.540 --> 10:13.460
And this introduces the foundational organizational principle of flow computing.

10:13.800 --> 10:15.700
It's called invocation through completion.

10:16.080 --> 10:17.340
Invocation through completion.

10:17.460 --> 10:17.680
Right.

10:18.080 --> 10:20.600
A process doesn't wake up because a clock told it to.

10:21.180 --> 10:23.360
It doesn't wake up because a scheduler commanded it.

10:23.520 --> 10:27.260
It becomes active only because its prerequisites are complete.

10:27.260 --> 10:29.220
I keep thinking about a relay race.

10:29.420 --> 10:30.220
Oh, that's a good one.

10:30.300 --> 10:38.400
Because in traditional computing, with the global clock, it's like there's a guy with a starting gun standing next to every single runner on the track.

10:38.680 --> 10:45.900
The first runner goes, stops, and then the guy looks at his stopwatch and fires a second gun for the second runner.

10:47.020 --> 10:50.240
Then he walks over and fires a third gun for the third runner.

10:50.440 --> 10:55.340
And if the first runner trips and falls, the guy with the stopwatch still fires the second gun anyway.

10:55.340 --> 11:00.380
Yes. And the second runner just runs without the baton, causing a total system failure.

11:00.700 --> 11:04.640
Exactly. But in flow computing, it's a real relay race.

11:05.160 --> 11:08.520
The runners don't wait for a gun for their specific leg of the race.

11:08.620 --> 11:09.520
They don't have a schedule.

11:09.760 --> 11:13.040
They just start running the exact second they feel that baton hit their hand.

11:13.140 --> 11:18.480
Yes. The completion of runner A's leg is the literal invocation of runner B's leg.

11:18.680 --> 11:19.360
So cool.

11:19.360 --> 11:27.400
And taking this out of hardware and looking at the bigger picture, this makes concurrency a primitive native feature of the system.

11:27.540 --> 11:29.360
Because the logic contains its own coordination.

11:30.020 --> 11:35.380
Right. You can have a thousand runners on a thousand different tracks, and you don't need a single starting gun.

11:35.720 --> 11:38.560
So the paper abstracts this away from electronic circuits, right?

11:38.920 --> 11:43.180
It gives these three universal rules for any flow-based system.

11:43.240 --> 11:45.240
Yes. Three rules. Let's go through them.

11:45.320 --> 11:45.840
Let's hear them.

11:45.840 --> 11:49.820
Rule one. Activate when your prerequisites are complete.

11:50.160 --> 11:51.620
You feel the baton, you run.

11:51.740 --> 11:57.120
Exactly. Rule two. Signal completion when your outputs are stable and correct.

11:57.320 --> 12:01.460
So you don't just hand off the baton, you make sure the next runner has a firm grip on it.

12:01.540 --> 12:06.480
Right. And rule three. Release your inputs when your downstream neighbor accepts your output.

12:06.800 --> 12:12.040
Meaning you let go of the baton and step off the track, resetting to a state of readiness for the next race.

12:12.420 --> 12:14.560
That's the return to the NUL state.

12:14.560 --> 12:16.140
That feels incredibly organic.

12:16.540 --> 12:23.280
But okay, let's bring this out of the theoretical realm of circuit boards and tracks and point it directly at the screen you're looking at right now.

12:23.340 --> 12:23.940
Let's do it.

12:24.000 --> 12:30.360
Because the paper takes this missing mathematician concept and applies it to our daily software use.

12:30.480 --> 12:33.180
It looks specifically at the application center desktop.

12:33.180 --> 12:36.000
The graphical user interface, the GUI.

12:36.160 --> 12:40.300
Right. The windows, the icons, the menus we've used for decades.

12:40.780 --> 12:45.700
The paper argues that the modern desktop fundamentally turns the user into the missing mathematician.

12:46.380 --> 12:50.780
I have to say, this was the moment in the paper where I felt personally attacked.

12:50.860 --> 12:51.960
It's a harsh realization.

12:52.160 --> 12:52.820
It really is.

12:52.820 --> 12:55.560
Think about a standard morning at the office.

12:56.460 --> 12:59.120
Your boss asks for a weekly summary report.

13:00.160 --> 13:03.120
To do this, you have to open a web browser to research your metrics.

13:03.620 --> 13:05.860
You have to open the word processor to write it.

13:06.080 --> 13:08.680
You have to actively click the button to run the skull checker.

13:08.800 --> 13:09.080
Yeah.

13:09.080 --> 13:15.100
Then you go to the file menu, click export, wait for the dialog box, choose PDF, and save it to your desktop.

13:15.540 --> 13:22.700
Then you open your email client, click compose, type the boss's address, find the file, attach it, and hit send.

13:22.840 --> 13:24.180
It's exhausting just hearing it.

13:24.220 --> 13:26.800
Right. The applications didn't coordinate any of that. You did.

13:26.960 --> 13:30.800
You are the frantic conductor pointing at the word processor and then pointing at the email client.

13:30.880 --> 13:33.800
You are literally enlivening passive applications.

13:33.800 --> 13:38.560
Without your constant manual intervention, the word processor does nothing.

13:39.100 --> 13:41.380
The email client sits perfectly still.

13:41.660 --> 13:50.300
And the paper outlines four massive compounding costs to putting the human user in this localized coordination role, doesn't it?

13:50.320 --> 13:53.300
It does. The first cost is variety of correctness.

13:53.660 --> 13:57.800
Meaning there is room for human error at every single manual transition.

13:58.280 --> 13:58.880
Exactly.

13:58.880 --> 14:06.260
Because you are managing the pipeline manually, the integrity of the data relies entirely on your memory and your attention span.

14:06.500 --> 14:07.360
Which is terrifying.

14:07.500 --> 14:09.440
You might forget to click the spell checker.

14:09.760 --> 14:13.580
You might accidentally attach last week's PDF instead of today's PDF.

14:14.080 --> 14:19.300
And the system has no way of knowing because the applications are completely isolated from each other.

14:19.560 --> 14:19.920
Wow.

14:20.220 --> 14:22.860
Okay, so the second cost is context switching overhead.

14:23.080 --> 14:23.300
Right.

14:23.300 --> 14:35.580
Finding the app in your Cask bar, clicking through the menus, remembering where the export button is hidden in this specific version of the software, getting your brain into the visual layout of the email client.

14:35.720 --> 14:37.160
And none of that is actual work.

14:37.280 --> 14:42.320
Right. That isn't creating the report. That is just the cognitive tax you pay for being the coordinator.

14:42.440 --> 14:43.300
It's pure overhead.

14:44.020 --> 14:48.540
Now, the third cost is the invisibility of intermediate representations.

14:49.080 --> 14:50.000
Meaning what exactly?

14:50.000 --> 14:56.720
When you do click save as PDF, the actual transformation process is completely hidden inside the application's black box.

14:56.800 --> 14:58.580
Oh, I see. You just see a loading bar, maybe?

14:58.940 --> 14:59.180
Yeah.

14:59.800 --> 15:01.780
But you can't inspect the wayfront halfway through.

15:02.000 --> 15:06.100
If the formatting breaks, you have no idea at what stage the calculation actually failed.

15:06.420 --> 15:07.400
It's invisible to you.

15:07.460 --> 15:08.460
That makes total sense.

15:08.720 --> 15:11.540
And the fourth cost is non-composability.

15:12.700 --> 15:14.040
This is the big one for me.

15:14.140 --> 15:15.180
It really is the killer.

15:15.180 --> 15:23.160
Like, I can't naturally pipe the output of my spell checker directly into a totally different application without manually copy and pasting it.

15:23.340 --> 15:25.620
The tools are trapped in their own little silos.

15:26.360 --> 15:29.340
Which brings us to the crucial distinction the paper makes.

15:30.400 --> 15:34.040
GUIs, visually speaking, are not inherently evil.

15:34.140 --> 15:36.080
Right. Humans are visual creatures.

15:36.240 --> 15:36.760
Of course.

15:37.120 --> 15:38.700
The problem is their encapsulation.

15:39.040 --> 15:42.320
GUIs trap processes behind visual terminuses.

15:42.620 --> 15:43.500
Visual terminuses.

15:43.500 --> 15:50.620
Yeah. A word processor does not easily expose its spell checking engine or its PDF rendering engine to the rest of your operating system.

15:51.060 --> 15:52.320
They are walled gardens.

15:52.860 --> 15:55.240
I hear that. But I have to push back a little here.

15:55.480 --> 15:58.680
For the sake of anyone who remembers life before Windows or Mac OS.

15:58.840 --> 15:59.380
Okay, go ahead.

15:59.920 --> 16:01.040
GUIs are intuitive.

16:01.820 --> 16:04.100
My parents can use an iPad. My kids can use an iPad.

16:04.740 --> 16:09.380
The alternative the paper brings up is Unix pipelines, which were built in the 1970s.

16:09.440 --> 16:09.660
True.

16:09.660 --> 16:20.200
So if the answer to the GY trap is that I have to use a command line Unix pipeline to send an email, don't I need a computer science degree just to function in modern society?

16:20.720 --> 16:21.820
It's a fair point.

16:21.820 --> 16:27.240
Right. And the paper acknowledges that the tradeoff for accessibility was necessary for the mass adoption of computers.

16:27.560 --> 16:27.660
Right.

16:28.140 --> 16:30.560
But we are talking about knowledge workers here.

16:31.020 --> 16:33.740
People who manipulate digital information for a living.

16:34.200 --> 16:40.740
For them, Unix pipelines are a practical software approximation of Carl Phan's logically determined flow.

16:41.060 --> 16:42.580
It's a way to stop being the mathematician.

16:42.580 --> 16:43.260
Exactly.

16:43.600 --> 16:48.040
Let's break down what a Unix pipeline actually is for someone who has never opened a terminal window.

16:48.320 --> 16:55.280
It's literally just plugging the output of one tiny single purpose program directly into the input of the next program.

16:55.480 --> 16:56.400
That's all it is.

16:56.680 --> 17:03.800
In Unix, a pipe, which is represented by that vertical bar character on your keyboard, is a completion governed coupling.

17:04.000 --> 17:05.440
A completion governed coupling.

17:05.780 --> 17:06.040
Yeah.

17:06.300 --> 17:07.920
You aren't managing a global state.

17:08.060 --> 17:11.700
You only need to understand what the paper calls behavioral neighborhoods.

17:11.700 --> 17:22.560
So if I have a folder full of a thousand text files, and I want to find the word flow in them, and then I want to sort those lines alphabetically, in a GY, I'd be opening files for hours.

17:22.660 --> 17:23.000
Oh, yeah.

17:23.320 --> 17:26.500
But in Unix, I run a command called cat to read the files.

17:26.760 --> 17:33.320
I pipe that output into a command called grep to find the word, and I pipe that output into a command called sort.

17:33.540 --> 17:35.040
Cat pipe, grep pipe sort.

17:35.520 --> 17:38.060
And here is where it maps perfectly onto flow computing.

17:38.520 --> 17:41.580
Text acts as the NUL convention of software here.

17:41.700 --> 17:42.060
Oh, wow.

17:42.200 --> 17:42.380
Okay.

17:42.560 --> 17:45.840
As cat reads the files, it streams the text to grep.

17:46.300 --> 17:49.520
Grep processes it as it arrives and streams it to sort.

17:49.720 --> 17:51.880
And when cat finishes the last file?

17:52.140 --> 17:54.560
It sends an invisible end of stream signal.

17:54.860 --> 17:57.600
That signal tells grep that the wavefront is complete.

17:57.820 --> 17:58.960
The baton has been passed.

17:59.080 --> 17:59.360
Yes.

18:00.100 --> 18:03.560
Grep finishes its work and sends its own end of stream signal to sort.

18:03.560 --> 18:07.800
And sort knows it has all the data, alphabetizes it, and prints it to the screen.

18:08.240 --> 18:08.760
Exactly.

18:09.020 --> 18:10.600
You didn't micromanage the timing.

18:10.840 --> 18:13.640
You didn't need a central clock telling grep when to start.

18:13.900 --> 18:18.880
The arrival of the data invoked the process, and the end of stream signal completed it.

18:19.260 --> 18:20.360
That is so elegant.

18:20.580 --> 18:21.100
It is.

18:21.160 --> 18:25.560
You don't need a computer science degree to understand a massive global architecture.

18:25.800 --> 18:29.900
You just need to understand the local neighborhood of the pipe you are using.

18:29.900 --> 18:30.900
What goes in?

18:30.900 --> 18:31.700
What comes out?

18:31.940 --> 18:32.180
Okay.

18:32.360 --> 18:37.380
So if GYs are this beautiful, comfortable trap that forces us to micromanage everything,

18:38.080 --> 18:43.020
and Unix pipes are the theoretical way out, how do we actually escape today?

18:43.220 --> 18:43.440
Right.

18:43.500 --> 18:46.320
Because most of us aren't working on mainframe computers in 1978.

18:46.700 --> 18:47.180
Exactly.

18:47.380 --> 18:50.100
How do we build a system on a modern laptop that actually flows?

18:50.700 --> 18:53.960
Well, the paper outlines what it calls a modern process ecology.

18:54.280 --> 18:55.240
Process ecology.

18:55.300 --> 18:55.560
Yeah.

18:55.560 --> 18:58.180
It isn't about abandoning modern tools entirely.

18:58.360 --> 18:59.400
It's about layering them.

18:59.400 --> 19:03.060
It gives a very specific software stack as a case study.

19:03.380 --> 19:04.000
Let's hear the stack.

19:04.120 --> 19:04.280
Okay.

19:05.060 --> 19:11.780
A Windows machine running WSL, the Windows subsystem for Linux, which gives you a native Ubuntu environment,

19:12.120 --> 19:19.160
Bash as the composition language to build the pipelines, Vim for text editing, Python for complex

19:19.160 --> 19:23.500
data manipulation, and critically, a program called AutoHotkey.

19:23.800 --> 19:24.360
AutoHotkey.

19:24.460 --> 19:28.260
I know gamers use this, and maybe people who do heavy data entry.

19:28.260 --> 19:29.220
Yeah, it's pretty common.

19:29.220 --> 19:33.020
But most people think of AutoHotkey as just a simple way to make keyboard shortcuts.

19:33.720 --> 19:39.040
Like, you program it so when you press Alt-M, it automatically types out your ridiculously

19:39.040 --> 19:42.840
long corporate email address so you don't have to type it 50 times a day.

19:42.920 --> 19:43.160
Right.

19:43.400 --> 19:45.740
But the paper reframes this tool completely.

19:45.940 --> 19:46.940
It elevates it.

19:47.060 --> 19:50.220
It calls AutoHotkey an ambient invocation vocabulary.

19:50.580 --> 19:52.280
An ambient invocation vocabulary.

19:52.420 --> 19:55.000
That phrasing is the key to the whole ecology, isn't it?

19:55.000 --> 19:55.700
It really is.

19:56.240 --> 19:59.920
In this setup, a hotkey isn't just a shortcut to a GUI destination.

20:00.460 --> 20:02.420
It is a named completion trigger.

20:02.820 --> 20:04.440
It fires the first wave front.

20:04.540 --> 20:04.820
Okay.

20:04.820 --> 20:05.780
Let's visualize this.

20:05.920 --> 20:06.040
Yeah.

20:06.100 --> 20:10.100
Let's say part of my job is taking a daily raw data dump, formatting it, and emailing it.

20:10.160 --> 20:10.340
Okay.

20:10.420 --> 20:11.420
Pretty standard task.

20:11.420 --> 20:13.540
In a flow ecology, I don't open Excel.

20:14.080 --> 20:17.260
I hit a custom hotkey, let's say Control-Alt-D for daily report.

20:17.540 --> 20:19.180
That hotkey acts as the baton.

20:19.700 --> 20:21.140
It triggers a bash script.

20:21.820 --> 20:24.840
The bash script runs a Python formatting tool on the raw data.

20:25.620 --> 20:31.200
The Python tool finishes and plips the clean data into a command line email client, and the

20:31.200 --> 20:32.100
email client sends it.

20:32.160 --> 20:33.840
And you untouch a single menu.

20:33.840 --> 20:39.680
The entire pipeline invokes itself through completion, triggered by one ambient keystroke.

20:40.500 --> 20:42.360
I didn't manage any of the intermediate steps.

20:42.440 --> 20:44.560
You've stepped completely out of the mathematician role.

20:44.920 --> 20:45.780
It's all automated.

20:45.860 --> 20:46.420
It's beautiful.

20:46.500 --> 20:49.500
But here is where the paper issues a very strong warning.

20:49.580 --> 20:50.060
A warning.

20:50.260 --> 20:50.560
Yes.

20:50.920 --> 20:55.480
When people hear about this kind of power, their first instinct is to try and automate absolutely

20:55.480 --> 20:56.860
everything immediately.

20:56.960 --> 20:57.420
Well, I've been there.

20:57.700 --> 21:01.400
The paper introduces a vital counter concept called earned automation.

21:01.780 --> 21:02.680
Earned automation.

21:02.680 --> 21:05.600
I have lived the failure of unearned automation.

21:05.660 --> 21:06.600
I think we all have.

21:06.660 --> 21:07.480
You get a new job.

21:07.580 --> 21:12.920
You spend three days writing a beautifully complex, incredibly fragile script to automate

21:12.920 --> 21:14.760
your entire workflow on day one.

21:14.880 --> 21:15.140
Right.

21:15.280 --> 21:21.100
And by day three, your boss slightly changes the format of the raw data dump, and your entire

21:21.100 --> 21:23.020
script shatters into a million pieces.

21:23.440 --> 21:23.620
Yep.

21:23.620 --> 21:28.660
You spend more time fixing the automation than you would have spent just doing the work

21:28.660 --> 21:29.180
manually.

21:29.500 --> 21:29.940
Exactly.

21:29.940 --> 21:34.760
You automated a surface task before the underlying process had actually stabilized.

21:35.500 --> 21:38.420
The process hadn't disclosed its true structure to you yet.

21:38.520 --> 21:39.620
That's a great way to say it.

21:39.700 --> 21:44.380
You hadn't discovered what the genuine completion relationships were or where the edge cases hid.

21:44.880 --> 21:50.540
The paper argues that in a flow ecology, manual repetition isn't just an inefficiency to

21:50.540 --> 21:51.360
be eradicated.

21:51.540 --> 21:52.220
What is it then?

21:52.420 --> 21:54.420
It is a period of vital observation.

21:54.760 --> 21:55.000
Okay.

21:55.000 --> 21:58.500
This reminds me of paving a dirt path on a university campus.

21:58.800 --> 21:59.420
Have you seen this?

21:59.500 --> 22:00.400
Oh, I love this analogy.

22:00.520 --> 22:00.760
Go ahead.

22:01.100 --> 22:05.760
An architect designs a beautiful quad with all these right-angled concrete sidewalks.

22:05.800 --> 22:07.500
They pour the concrete on day one.

22:08.080 --> 22:12.240
But humans are lazy, or rather, humans seek the path of least resistance.

22:12.640 --> 22:12.880
Naturally.

22:12.880 --> 22:17.780
So the students just cut diagonally across the grass to get to the dining hall, because

22:17.780 --> 22:18.300
it's faster.

22:19.000 --> 22:24.000
And after a year, there's this ugly dirt trench right through the grass, and the expensive

22:24.000 --> 22:25.340
concrete is ignored.

22:25.520 --> 22:30.100
Because the architect tried to dictate the flow before observing the reality of the environment.

22:30.820 --> 22:31.260
Exactly.

22:31.820 --> 22:33.940
The smart universities do the opposite.

22:34.460 --> 22:37.300
They build the buildings, and they plant grass everywhere.

22:37.560 --> 22:38.300
No sidewalks.

22:38.300 --> 22:38.680
They wait.

22:38.680 --> 22:39.680
They wait a year.

22:40.060 --> 22:41.100
They let the students walk.

22:41.220 --> 22:44.520
They let the dirt paths naturally form based on where people actually need to go.

22:45.000 --> 22:49.060
And then once the route is stabilized by thousands of footsteps, they pour the concrete

22:49.060 --> 22:50.320
over the dirt paths.

22:50.600 --> 22:51.680
They earn the automation.

22:51.900 --> 22:52.680
They earn the automation.

22:52.860 --> 22:58.320
Because if you automate prematurely, as the paper elegantly puts it, you harden confusion

22:58.320 --> 22:59.100
into software.

22:59.500 --> 23:00.960
Harden confusion into software.

23:01.220 --> 23:01.520
No.

23:01.760 --> 23:05.400
You pour concrete over an inefficient or misunderstood process.

23:05.400 --> 23:10.500
The goal of flow computing isn't just to be hyperproductive with brittle scripts.

23:11.140 --> 23:14.800
True flow literacy requires the discipline to delay automation.

23:15.200 --> 23:20.620
So you perform a process manually in the terminal, stringing the commands together one by one,

23:20.840 --> 23:22.620
watching the output, seeing what breaks.

23:22.780 --> 23:23.100
Right.

23:23.320 --> 23:27.600
And the paper talks about Vim macros as the perfect tool for this observation phase.

23:28.000 --> 23:30.680
It calls them deferred process crystallization.

23:31.000 --> 23:32.760
Deferred process crystallization.

23:32.760 --> 23:35.320
So a macro is basically a provisional expression.

23:35.660 --> 23:35.940
Yes.

23:36.660 --> 23:38.720
Let's say you are cleaning up a list of games.

23:39.160 --> 23:40.380
You hit record in Vim.

23:40.480 --> 23:41.620
You delete the middle initial.

23:41.880 --> 23:42.580
You add a comma.

23:42.880 --> 23:44.420
You capitalize the last name.

23:44.500 --> 23:45.080
You hit stop.

23:45.160 --> 23:45.400
Okay.

23:46.060 --> 23:48.280
You have recorded a sequence of transformations.

23:48.720 --> 23:51.960
It's repeatable, but it's not yet a permanent script.

23:52.120 --> 23:54.480
It hasn't been given a permanent name in your system.

23:54.640 --> 23:55.180
It's a dirt path.

23:55.500 --> 23:56.240
It is a dirt path.

23:56.400 --> 24:00.500
You can test that Vim macro 10,000 times on your data set.

24:00.500 --> 24:05.200
If it breaks on a name with a hyphen, you realize your process wasn't fully understood.

24:05.520 --> 24:06.220
You revise it.

24:06.440 --> 24:08.640
And if it works perfectly, it was 10,000 times?

24:08.840 --> 24:10.840
Then the completion relationships hold.

24:11.180 --> 24:12.380
The path is stable.

24:13.080 --> 24:17.520
Only then has it earned its promotion to a permanent Python script or an auto-hockey trigger.

24:17.780 --> 24:18.780
You pour the concrete.

24:19.040 --> 24:25.300
And over time, your auto-hockey configuration file becomes this amazing, deeply personal artifact.

24:25.840 --> 24:27.000
It's not just a list of settings.

24:27.000 --> 24:29.960
The paper calls it a fossil record of process discovery.

24:30.120 --> 24:30.820
A fossil record.

24:30.920 --> 24:31.420
I love that.

24:31.980 --> 24:37.740
Every single hot key in there is a process that you did manually, observed, tested, stabilized,

24:38.160 --> 24:39.460
and finally gave a name to.

24:39.880 --> 24:44.260
It's a literal map of your own evolving understanding of your work over years.

24:44.500 --> 24:47.680
Which represents a profound shift from the application mindset.

24:48.280 --> 24:53.160
In the GI world, your vocabulary is just a list of nouns, the names of the apps you happen

24:53.160 --> 24:53.580
to own.

24:54.120 --> 24:55.960
Word, Excel, Photoshop.

24:55.960 --> 24:57.460
But in a flow ecology.

24:57.520 --> 25:02.320
In a flow ecology, your vocabulary is entirely made up of verbs of process activation.

25:03.100 --> 25:04.660
You don't think I need to open Word.

25:05.060 --> 25:10.060
You type a command that means generate overview, and the system coordinates whatever transformations

25:10.060 --> 25:11.700
are necessary to make that happen.

25:12.080 --> 25:15.820
This all sounds incredible for text files and data sorting.

25:16.180 --> 25:22.140
But let's take this methodology and point it at the absolute messy cutting edge of today's

25:22.140 --> 25:22.620
technology.

25:22.660 --> 25:23.780
Oh, this is the best part.

25:23.780 --> 25:28.500
Because everyone is talking about large language models, AI, ChatGPT, Claude.

25:29.160 --> 25:31.720
How does flow handle something so complex?

25:31.860 --> 25:37.360
Well, the paper points out that how we currently interact with AI is the ultimate inescapable

25:37.360 --> 25:38.260
GUI trap.

25:38.260 --> 25:41.720
It really is the pinnacle of the missing mathematician problem, isn't it?

25:41.720 --> 25:42.600
Think about it.

25:42.600 --> 25:47.580
When you use a web interface for an LLM, you are doing all the connective tissue work.

25:48.080 --> 25:50.180
You copy the text from your document.

25:50.780 --> 25:51.820
You switch to the browser.

25:52.320 --> 25:53.700
You paste it into the chat box.

25:53.860 --> 25:55.280
You manually write the prompt.

25:55.820 --> 25:56.700
You hit enter.

25:56.860 --> 25:57.900
You wait for it to generate.

25:57.900 --> 25:59.980
You evaluate the output visually.

26:00.340 --> 26:01.400
You copy the response.

26:01.620 --> 26:02.780
You switch back to your document.

26:03.020 --> 26:03.900
And you paste it.

26:03.980 --> 26:04.660
It's exhausting.

26:05.220 --> 26:11.680
The AI's immense cognitive capability is entirely locked inside an application silo that requires

26:11.680 --> 26:14.300
your manual coordination for every single interaction.

26:14.620 --> 26:18.500
So how do we pull AI out of the browser and into the flow?

26:18.500 --> 26:21.420
By integrating local models directly into the pipeline.

26:22.000 --> 26:26.160
The paper uses the examples of Whisper, which is an audio transcription model, and Alama,

26:26.460 --> 26:29.300
a tool for running LLMs locally on your own hardware.

26:29.480 --> 26:29.680
Right.

26:29.960 --> 26:32.220
In a flow ecology, these aren't apps.

26:32.480 --> 26:33.780
They are command line tools.

26:33.960 --> 26:37.280
They become what the paper calls cognitive operator boundaries.

26:37.580 --> 26:38.680
They become just another pipe.

26:38.980 --> 26:44.900
So instead of all that copying and pasting, my pipeline is literally take this audio file

26:44.900 --> 26:48.060
from a meeting, flow it into Whisper to get the text.

26:48.500 --> 26:54.600
Flow that text directly into Alama with a prompt asking it to extract the action items.

26:54.800 --> 26:54.880
Yep.

26:55.100 --> 27:00.680
Flow those action items into Pandoc to format them as an HTML webpage and flow that page

27:00.680 --> 27:04.500
into Git to publish it to the team's internal server.

27:04.620 --> 27:05.020
Boom.

27:05.360 --> 27:05.580
Boom.

27:05.920 --> 27:09.520
I hit one hotkey and five minutes later, the meeting notes are published.

27:09.720 --> 27:09.880
Yeah.

27:09.980 --> 27:11.700
No human mathematician needed.

27:11.820 --> 27:14.140
It is a stunning capability.

27:14.140 --> 27:20.040
But integrating probabilistic AI models into deterministic automated pipelines introduces

27:20.040 --> 27:22.400
a massive critical new challenge.

27:22.560 --> 27:23.000
Oh, right.

27:23.220 --> 27:24.580
Because AI isn't perfect.

27:24.700 --> 27:24.820
No.

27:24.920 --> 27:26.820
The paper calls it the weak completion problem.

27:26.900 --> 27:27.780
The weak completion problem.

27:27.960 --> 27:30.900
Walk me through this because this seems like the Achilles heel of the whole system.

27:31.160 --> 27:32.500
It could be if you don't manage it.

27:32.920 --> 27:34.560
In traditional Unix, a pipe is binary.

27:34.840 --> 27:38.060
A command like sort or grep either works perfectly or it crashes.

27:38.060 --> 27:42.180
If it works, it passes the data forward and sends an exit code of zero.

27:42.720 --> 27:47.940
The exit code of zero is the universal signal that says, my output is structurally sound.

27:48.100 --> 27:48.860
You can trust it.

27:49.260 --> 27:51.520
But AI isn't deterministic like sort.

27:51.700 --> 27:52.080
Exactly.

27:52.520 --> 27:56.860
An LLM can produce output that is syntactically perfectly correct.

27:57.080 --> 27:57.900
It is valid text.

27:58.000 --> 27:58.920
The program didn't crash.

27:59.000 --> 27:59.920
The exit code is zero.

28:00.180 --> 28:03.140
But the content is semantically inadmissible.

28:03.440 --> 28:04.200
It hallucinates.

28:04.220 --> 28:04.880
It hallucinates.

28:04.960 --> 28:06.280
It formats the data wrong.

28:06.280 --> 28:08.000
It drops half the transcript.

28:08.320 --> 28:08.520
Yes.

28:08.960 --> 28:14.400
The candidate output looks exactly like a completed wave front to the operating system, but it isn't.

28:14.480 --> 28:21.160
So the downstream process, which is blindly expecting real formatted data, can't just trust the exit code anymore.

28:21.280 --> 28:21.960
No, it can't.

28:22.000 --> 28:24.780
It's like having a really smooth-talking, well-dressed employee.

28:25.000 --> 28:25.200
Yeah.

28:25.260 --> 28:28.520
You ask them for a highly detailed financial report by 5 p.m.

28:28.520 --> 28:28.780
Okay.

28:29.100 --> 28:35.320
At 459, they walk into your office and hand you a beautifully bound, perfectly formatted leather folder.

28:35.560 --> 28:36.000
Nice.

28:36.280 --> 28:37.100
Exit code zero.

28:37.560 --> 28:39.020
They did the job on time.

28:39.060 --> 28:39.260
Yeah.

28:39.420 --> 28:42.480
But then you open it, and all the math inside is entirely fabricated.

28:42.600 --> 28:43.700
They hallucinated the profits.

28:43.920 --> 28:52.140
If you just take that folder and blindly hand it to the board of directors, which is what a pipeline does, you ruin the company.

28:52.220 --> 28:53.260
You absolutely do.

28:53.260 --> 29:01.780
So if the AI can hallucinate, isn't it wildly irresponsible to put it in an automated flow where it just passes its lies to the next step?

29:01.780 --> 29:04.520
This is where flow computing requires us to evolve.

29:04.520 --> 29:09.680
The paper solution to the smooth-talking employee is the admissibility functional.

29:09.680 --> 29:10.960
Admissibility functional.

29:10.960 --> 29:11.620
What does that do?

29:11.620 --> 29:14.300
You don't just accept the output of an AI node.

29:14.620 --> 29:21.880
You intercept it and evaluate it against four distinct levels of completion before allowing the wavefront to continue.

29:21.880 --> 29:23.020
Okay, let's build one.

29:23.020 --> 29:29.080
Let's say I want my LLM to output a list of names in a very specific JSON format so a database can read it.

29:29.080 --> 29:31.540
Okay, level one is basic exit code completion.

29:31.900 --> 29:33.360
Did the ELLAMA process crash?

29:33.520 --> 29:34.900
Did your computer run out of memory?

29:35.180 --> 29:36.100
If yes, halt.

29:36.260 --> 29:36.440
Right.

29:36.720 --> 29:38.920
If it returns a zero, we move to level two.

29:39.120 --> 29:40.620
Level two is format completion.

29:40.940 --> 29:41.180
Right.

29:41.720 --> 29:47.920
We take the LLM's output and we pipe it into a tiny validation tool, maybe a JSON parser, like JKEY.

29:48.760 --> 29:57.020
We ask, is this actually valid JSON, or did the AI include a bunch of conversational filler like, here is the JSON you requested?

29:57.380 --> 29:58.320
Which they always do.

29:58.320 --> 29:58.920
Always.

29:59.480 --> 30:02.340
And if the parser chokes on the text, the format completion fails?

30:02.540 --> 30:05.660
But even if it is perfect JSON, it might still be garbage data.

30:05.760 --> 30:09.440
Which brings us to level three, semantic completion.

30:10.380 --> 30:12.260
Did it actually answer the prompt?

30:12.320 --> 30:13.720
How do we check that automatically?

30:13.840 --> 30:16.260
This requires a more complex admissibility test.

30:16.620 --> 30:22.400
Maybe you run a script that checks if the JSON contains at least five names, or if the names match a certain rejects pattern.

30:22.740 --> 30:25.740
You are verifying the meaning of the data, not just the shape.

30:25.900 --> 30:26.240
Got it.

30:26.280 --> 30:27.080
And the final level.

30:27.080 --> 30:29.800
Level four is ecological completion.

30:30.360 --> 30:31.580
This is the ultimate test.

30:31.580 --> 30:36.860
Is this output actually useful as a starting condition for the next specific step in your system?

30:37.100 --> 30:37.780
Give me an example.

30:37.780 --> 30:45.100
If the next step requires the names to cross-reference with an existing employee database, the admissibility functional might do a quick database dip.

30:45.600 --> 30:50.500
If the names the AI generated don't exist in the company, the ecological completion fails.

30:50.500 --> 30:56.240
So the flow forces us to build repair or quarantine loops directly into the ecology.

30:56.780 --> 30:58.700
It isn't just a straight line anymore.

30:58.820 --> 30:59.880
It's a living system.

31:00.020 --> 31:00.760
Very much so.

31:00.880 --> 31:07.740
If the AI output fails the admissibility test at any of those four levels, the biplane doesn't just crash and burn.

31:07.880 --> 31:08.880
It could loop back.

31:09.100 --> 31:09.460
Exactly.

31:09.460 --> 31:15.820
And automatically reprompt the LLM, passing the error message back to it saying, you gave me invalid JSON, try again.

31:15.920 --> 31:23.060
Or if it fails three times, it can quarantine the file into a suspect folder for a human to review while the rest of the system keeps running.

31:23.060 --> 31:29.920
It manages the inherently probabilistic, messy nature of AI with the rigorous elegance of flow logic.

31:30.060 --> 31:31.340
It really is a brilliant synthesis.

31:31.580 --> 31:31.780
Okay.

31:31.960 --> 31:33.540
So the pipeline runs successfully.

31:33.880 --> 31:36.080
The admissibility functional approves the output.

31:36.580 --> 31:38.700
The wavelength propagates all the way to the end.

31:39.120 --> 31:39.980
It creates data.

31:40.380 --> 31:46.740
But flow computing asks us to completely rethink what a file actually is once that process finishes.

31:46.740 --> 31:54.540
We usually think of a file as a storage container, a passive box you put data into so you don't lose it when you turn the computer off.

31:54.640 --> 31:54.760
Right.

31:55.040 --> 31:57.740
You name it, you put it in a folder, and it sits there, inert.

31:58.420 --> 32:02.740
But the paper states that in a flow ecology, files are captured wavefronts.

32:03.400 --> 32:07.240
Or frozen Markov boundaries, which sounds incredibly intimidating.

32:07.360 --> 32:08.160
Let's unpack that.

32:08.240 --> 32:13.300
It sounds like a lot, but a Markov boundary is just a concept from statistics and information theory.

32:13.300 --> 32:18.080
The simplest way to understand a Markov system is that it has a kind of amnesia.

32:18.420 --> 32:18.900
Amnesia.

32:19.000 --> 32:19.260
Yes.

32:19.600 --> 32:25.500
The future state of the system depends only on the current state, and completely ignores the history of how it got there.

32:25.640 --> 32:30.160
So if I'm playing a board game, my next move only depends on where the pieces are right now.

32:30.640 --> 32:33.940
It doesn't matter what moves I made three turns ago to get them there.

32:34.320 --> 32:36.200
The current board state is the boundary.

32:36.760 --> 32:37.220
Exactly.

32:37.220 --> 32:43.780
In this ecology, a file on your hard drive is the physical, frozen evidence that a wavefront reached a completion boundary.

32:44.160 --> 32:46.800
The paper uses a much more evocative term for this, though.

32:47.080 --> 32:47.740
Which is?

32:47.940 --> 32:48.460
Residue.

32:48.700 --> 32:49.180
Residue.

32:49.360 --> 32:51.200
I am obsessed with this reframing.

32:51.600 --> 32:53.220
It's the memory of a completed wavefront.

32:53.980 --> 32:54.340
Yes.

32:54.560 --> 32:55.860
Think about an audio transcript.

32:56.480 --> 33:00.300
In the GUI world, it's just a document you open in Word.

33:01.500 --> 33:05.420
But in flow computing, it is the residue of the transcription process.

33:05.420 --> 33:16.900
It's very existence proves the transcription wavefront has completed, and it acts as the exact invocation condition that allows the downstream summarization process to begin.

33:17.180 --> 33:20.860
This redefines how we interact with our terminals entirely.

33:21.060 --> 33:22.080
Think about tab completion.

33:22.340 --> 33:22.600
Okay.

33:22.880 --> 33:27.080
When you are typing in the command line, you hit the tab key, and it auto-completes the file name.

33:27.220 --> 33:28.380
Yeah, I do that all the time.

33:28.620 --> 33:32.700
Usually, we just view that as a typing convenience, so we don't have to spell out a long file name.

33:32.700 --> 33:38.480
But the paper elevates tab completion and checking your shell history as acts of ecological sensing.

33:38.740 --> 33:39.500
Ecological sensing.

33:40.000 --> 33:42.800
Because the golden rule here is discover before produce.

33:42.960 --> 33:43.220
Right.

33:43.300 --> 33:47.020
You don't just blindly fire off a command because it's the next step on your checklist.

33:47.300 --> 33:49.200
You check the environment for residue first.

33:49.660 --> 33:55.180
You type whisper and hit tab to see if transcript.txt already exists in the directory.

33:55.780 --> 33:56.260
Exactly.

33:56.260 --> 34:01.160
And if it does, you don't need to run the expensive, time-consuming transcription model again.

34:01.420 --> 34:02.500
You just use the residue.

34:02.720 --> 34:11.260
It changes files from passive storage boxes into active ecological memory, and this enables what the paper calls second-order flow computing.

34:12.020 --> 34:12.780
Feedback loops.

34:13.000 --> 34:13.280
Yes.

34:13.980 --> 34:15.140
Think about software development.

34:15.640 --> 34:16.720
You write a piece of code.

34:17.100 --> 34:17.920
You run the compiler.

34:18.180 --> 34:20.420
The compiler fails and spits out an error log.

34:20.620 --> 34:20.860
Right.

34:20.860 --> 34:23.640
In flow computing, that error log is residue.

34:24.100 --> 34:26.500
It is a captured wavefront of a failed process.

34:27.100 --> 34:29.580
That residue then modifies your next action.

34:29.980 --> 34:34.820
You read the log, you edit the code based on the specific error, and you invoke the compiler again.

34:34.980 --> 34:38.140
The system is evolving based on its own accumulating residue.

34:38.400 --> 34:42.140
The output of the flow changes the parameters of the next flow.

34:42.460 --> 34:44.100
Let me make sure I'm wrapping my head around this.

34:44.160 --> 34:48.660
I think about traditional file saving like throwing boxes into a dusty, disorganized attic.

34:48.660 --> 34:55.420
You put a box up there, you write taxes 2024 on it, you hope you can find it later, but it's basically just dead weight.

34:55.940 --> 35:04.400
But in flow computing, leaving a residue file is like leaving a baton sitting on a very specific marked spot on a relay track.

35:04.480 --> 35:05.120
I like that.

35:05.500 --> 35:12.140
The second, the next runner is ready, whether that runner arrives five minutes later or five months later, they know exactly where the baton is.

35:12.140 --> 35:19.320
And the mere physical presence of the baton on the track gives them the permission and the data they need to start running.

35:20.040 --> 35:23.440
And this brings us back to the power of the frozen Markov boundary.

35:23.800 --> 35:29.240
Because of that statistical amnesia we talked about, once that transcript is complete and frozen as a residue file,

35:29.500 --> 35:34.540
the downstream summarization process doesn't need to know anything about the history of how it was made.

35:34.680 --> 35:35.980
It isolates the complexity.

35:35.980 --> 35:36.700
Completely.

35:37.140 --> 35:44.940
The summarization AI doesn't care if you recorded the audio on a $10,000 studio microphone or a broken cell phone.

35:45.140 --> 35:45.260
Right.

35:45.420 --> 35:51.140
It doesn't care if Whisper transcribed it in 10 seconds on a supercomputer or took two hours on an old laptop.

35:51.700 --> 35:56.260
It doesn't care if the transcription crashed three times and was repaired by an admissibility functional.

35:56.820 --> 35:59.180
All of that chaotic history is erased at the boundary.

35:59.600 --> 36:00.160
Exactly.

36:00.440 --> 36:04.800
The file is a perfect, clean separator between computational episodes.

36:04.800 --> 36:06.280
That is genuinely beautiful.

36:06.440 --> 36:08.920
It stops errors from compounding across the system.

36:09.080 --> 36:09.700
It really does.

36:09.820 --> 36:09.960
Okay.

36:10.040 --> 36:17.260
We've zoomed way in on Unix pipelines, AI operator boundaries, and files as frozen Markov boundaries.

36:17.500 --> 36:20.480
Let's zoom all the way back out to the human interface.

36:20.540 --> 36:20.760
Sure.

36:20.900 --> 36:22.180
Because my brain is starting to hurt.

36:22.880 --> 36:29.560
How do we actually navigate a world built on thousands of cascading wave fronts and residues without losing our minds?

36:29.880 --> 36:33.480
Because as a knowledge worker, I have 50 different projects going on at once.

36:33.600 --> 36:34.100
We all do.

36:34.100 --> 36:39.100
The paper's answer to navigating this chaos is nested scopes.

36:39.260 --> 36:39.520
Yes.

36:39.820 --> 36:48.160
The paper introduces tools like Biobu, which is a terminal multiplexer, VIM modes, and a theoretical model it calls the SpherePop Calculus.

36:48.720 --> 36:49.800
SpherePop Calculus.

36:49.920 --> 36:52.320
That sounds like a discarded title for a Willy Wonka invention.

36:52.640 --> 36:53.040
What is it?

36:53.600 --> 36:58.620
The underlying math isn't as important as the core philosophical concept, which is scope before process.

36:58.820 --> 36:59.680
Scope before process.

36:59.680 --> 37:06.920
In a traditional GUI operating system, the process, the application itself, is the center of the universe.

37:07.680 --> 37:11.780
The window that the application lives in is just a dumb, transparent container.

37:12.020 --> 37:12.200
Right.

37:12.440 --> 37:15.300
But flow computing reverses this hierarchy entirely.

37:15.860 --> 37:18.100
The scope, the behavioral container, comes first.

37:18.520 --> 37:20.280
The container dictates reality.

37:20.280 --> 37:23.720
Give me a practical example of the container dictating reality.

37:24.120 --> 37:26.040
You mentioned VIM earlier.

37:26.280 --> 37:27.460
VIM operates on modes.

37:28.020 --> 37:31.720
When you are in insert mode, the scope is defined as text entry.

37:32.040 --> 37:34.720
If you press the letter D, it types a D on the screen.

37:34.840 --> 37:35.060
Okay.

37:35.060 --> 37:41.380
But if you hit escape and shift the scope into normal mode, the local behavioral neighborhood completely changes.

37:41.680 --> 37:44.320
Now, if you press the letter D, it doesn't type anything.

37:44.620 --> 37:46.340
It deletes a word or a line.

37:46.440 --> 37:47.740
The keystroke is identical.

37:48.140 --> 37:50.020
The container dictated the action.

37:50.240 --> 37:56.860
And Bioboo takes this concept of nested behavioral containers and scales it up to your entire workspace.

37:57.100 --> 37:57.280
Right.

37:57.280 --> 38:01.020
Instead of 50 chaotic windows floating around a desktop, you have a strict hierarchy.

38:01.360 --> 38:06.080
You have panes, which are small bubbles holding a single shell process like a running script.

38:06.400 --> 38:11.680
Panes live inside windows, which are larger bubbles organizing a shared task like database maintenance.

38:12.220 --> 38:17.020
And windows live inside sessions, which are massive bubbles containing a whole project.

38:17.020 --> 38:24.480
Each bubble, each scope, provides its own isolated context, its own specific history, and its own local residue.

38:24.480 --> 38:32.880
You can detach from a session, go home, log in from a different computer, reattach to the session, and the entire scope is exactly as you left it.

38:33.140 --> 38:34.800
The environment is the interface.

38:35.040 --> 38:35.300
Yes.

38:35.780 --> 38:43.980
And all of this, the missing mathematician, the wavefronts, the pipelines, the nested scopes, it all builds to the grand thesis of the paper.

38:44.020 --> 38:46.420
The ultimate goal of adopting flow computing.

38:46.680 --> 38:48.800
The return of what it calls process literacy.

38:48.800 --> 38:56.440
This is the historical and sociological comparison that elevates this paper from a technical manual to a philosophical treatise.

38:56.580 --> 38:57.340
It really is profound.

38:57.540 --> 39:03.060
For the vast majority of human history, skilled workers understood their domains entirely through completion relationships.

39:03.620 --> 39:05.360
They had innate process literacy.

39:05.600 --> 39:07.420
The paper uses the example of a farmer.

39:08.180 --> 39:11.880
A skilled farmer doesn't just walk out into a field and push a giant grow food button.

39:12.000 --> 39:12.660
No, they don't.

39:12.660 --> 39:15.540
They understand the intricate flow of the harvest cycles.

39:16.120 --> 39:24.080
They know that a specific sequence of temperature changes and soil moisture levels is the prerequisite completion event required to plant seeds.

39:24.240 --> 39:24.480
Right.

39:24.660 --> 39:29.960
They know exactly what visual and tactile cues signal the completion boundary for harvesting the crop.

39:29.960 --> 39:39.480
And they know that the decaying stalks left behind in the field are the physical residue that enlivens the soil, providing the starting conditions for next year's wavefront.

39:39.820 --> 39:43.260
Or consider a master machinist working with metal.

39:43.360 --> 39:45.920
They don't just put raw steel into a black box.

39:45.980 --> 39:46.420
Exactly.

39:46.580 --> 39:51.560
They understand exactly which roughing operations are prerequisites for the finishing operations.

39:51.980 --> 39:55.960
They understand what microscopic tolerances constitute genuine completion.

39:55.960 --> 40:02.100
They understand the physical flow of the material through the shop floor, from raw stock to finished engine block.

40:02.400 --> 40:05.440
They possess deep structural literacy of their medium.

40:06.020 --> 40:09.960
But then, the graphical user interface happened to computing in the 1980s.

40:10.800 --> 40:11.360
It did.

40:11.720 --> 40:14.340
And the paper makes this devastating argument.

40:14.780 --> 40:17.520
The GOI traded process literacy for accessibility.

40:18.060 --> 40:22.620
We purposefully created an entire generation of computer users.

40:22.620 --> 40:35.080
And I put myself firmly in this category, by the way, who know exactly which brightly colored icon to click, but who have absolutely no idea what completion events govern the transformations happening behind the glass screen.

40:35.480 --> 40:38.200
We traded structural understanding for ease of use.

40:38.780 --> 40:44.280
We encapsulated the complexity so completely that the user became blind to the flow.

40:44.560 --> 40:48.860
It's the difference between driving a manual transmission car and using a ride-sharing app.

40:48.920 --> 40:50.140
Oh, that's a perfect comparison.

40:50.140 --> 40:54.600
In the manual car, you are intimately connected to the mechanical flow of the machine.

40:55.060 --> 40:56.600
You hear the RPMs rise.

40:56.840 --> 40:58.760
You physically engage the clutch.

40:58.860 --> 41:00.580
You choose the gear based on the terrain.

41:00.860 --> 41:03.700
You have process literacy of the vehicle's momentum.

41:04.140 --> 41:04.260
Right.

41:04.440 --> 41:07.400
But with a ride-sharing app, you literally just tap a glass screen.

41:07.620 --> 41:08.780
A car magically appears.

41:08.960 --> 41:09.960
You get in the back seat.

41:10.160 --> 41:12.160
And you stare at your phone until you arrive.

41:12.540 --> 41:14.800
You have no idea what route the driver took.

41:15.040 --> 41:17.600
You have no idea how an internal combustion engine works.

41:17.600 --> 41:20.500
You traded literacy for the ultimate convenience.

41:20.940 --> 41:24.860
Which, to be fair, is perfectly fine if your only goal is to get to the grocery store.

41:25.160 --> 41:27.300
But wait, I have to challenge this premise again.

41:27.380 --> 41:27.520
Yeah.

41:28.040 --> 41:30.680
Isn't that accessibility a monumental triumph?

41:31.240 --> 41:33.400
I don't want to know how the engine works to get groceries.

41:33.780 --> 41:39.500
I don't want to learn bash scripting and pipe text through command line tools just to write an email to my team.

41:39.720 --> 41:40.320
I get that.

41:40.440 --> 41:42.120
The GUI democratized computing.

41:42.120 --> 41:49.460
It is a totally valid challenge, and the paper does not argue that we should force everyone back to command lines for basic tasks.

41:49.660 --> 41:52.280
The trade-off was necessary for mass adoption.

41:52.460 --> 41:52.600
Right.

41:52.780 --> 42:09.200
But it argues forcefully that for knowledge workers, people whose entire professional value is derived from manipulating, transforming, and synthesizing digital information remaining functionally illiterate about the processes they use every day, is a massive self-imposed limitation on their potential.

42:09.200 --> 42:15.520
Because if you don't know how the engine works, you can only drive on the roads that the app developers explicitly built for you.

42:15.780 --> 42:16.240
Precisely.

42:16.480 --> 42:23.000
If you only know how to tap the glass and navigate menus, you are permanently trapped inside someone else's encapsulation.

42:23.380 --> 42:28.540
You can only combine tools if a tech giant decided to build a specific integration for them.

42:29.020 --> 42:31.200
But if you possess process literacy...

42:31.200 --> 42:42.280
If you understand what prerequisite each process requires, what residue it deposits, and what future invocations it enables, you gain true computational autonomy.

42:42.520 --> 42:43.860
You can compose your own boundaries.

42:44.140 --> 42:44.300
Yeah.

42:44.340 --> 42:56.400
If I have process literacy, I can chain an audio transcription directly into a local language model, pipe the output into a custom admissibility script to check for hallucinations, and pipe that into a web server to publish it.

42:56.400 --> 42:56.840
Yes.

42:56.840 --> 43:05.800
I can build a machine perfectly tailored to my specific brain without waiting for a Silicon Valley startup to invent an app that does exactly that.

43:06.300 --> 43:11.120
I stop being the exhausted manager of their tools, and I become the architect of my own flow.

43:11.340 --> 43:13.160
We have covered immense ground today.

43:13.240 --> 43:13.820
We really have.

43:13.880 --> 43:19.560
We started by diagnosing that feeling of cognitive fatigue, tracing it all the way back to Carl Phantz, missing mathematician.

43:20.120 --> 43:25.480
The realization that we are the ones manually enlivening dead logic because the clock forces us to.

43:25.480 --> 43:32.740
We looked at the alternative, replacing rigid, clocked states with wave fronts of data that invoke themselves through completion.

43:33.220 --> 43:42.060
We saw how the EUI forces us to pay the massive costs of context switching and hidden representations, and how Unix pipelines offer a logically determined way out.

43:42.060 --> 43:58.040
We explored how to build a modern process ecology using tools like WSL, BASH, and VIM, and why we must use auto hotkey not as a shortcut, but to enforce earned automation, allowing the dirt paths of our habits to stabilize before pouring the concrete.

43:58.040 --> 44:06.020
We confronted the terrifying weak completion problem of integrating AI, learning that we can't just trust a smooth-talking LLM.

44:06.420 --> 44:12.300
We have to treat them as cognitive operator boundaries, heavily gated by multilevel admissibility tests.

44:12.640 --> 44:23.840
We reimagined the files sitting on our hard drives not as dusty storage boxes, but as captured wave fronts frozen Markov boundaries that leave behind powerful ecological residue for the next process to discover.

44:23.840 --> 44:31.760
And finally, we saw how nested scopes help us organize this ecology, all in the service of reclaiming our process literacy from the comfort of the EUI trial.

44:32.100 --> 44:35.080
It is a radical proposition for how to live in a digital world.

44:35.080 --> 44:47.300
So I want you to do something right now. Look at your own desktop. Look at the icons, the open tabs, the notifications waiting for you. Ask yourself, are you the missing mathematician in your own digital life?

44:47.940 --> 44:55.360
Are you the exhausted conductor waving a baton at a silent, uncoordinated orchestra, desperately trying to make them play a symphony?

44:55.520 --> 45:00.380
Are you spending your days managing the connective tissue, or are you actually doing the work?

45:00.380 --> 45:05.100
I'm going to leave you with a final thought to ponder, something that builds on everything we've unpacked today.

45:05.740 --> 45:11.800
What if the feeling of information overload you experience isn't actually a problem of having too much data?

45:12.340 --> 45:23.360
What if it's just the cognitive fatigue of acting as the manual external coordinator for a thousand uncompleted digital wave fronts that are desperately waiting for a logically determined system to set them free?

45:24.040 --> 45:27.120
We'll let you sit with that. Thanks for joining us on this deep dive.

